  "note": "spec/rust_conformance_spec.rb's own fixture — the NAMED/declared bluebook query, the STRING form of the \"query\" step (kernel/cli.rs's own header on the two shapes), proven for real for every real declared query in the corpus that `rust/project/queries.rb`'s eligibility gate actually admits: Banking's own CardPayment.Pending/Disputed/Flagged and ExternalTransfer.Sent (all wheres-only, no order_by/limit), plus Governance's RoleAssignment.AssignmentsForActor and RoleTransition.Allowed and Identity's ExternalIdentifier.ResolvedBy — three framework-chapter queries reachable here only because banking.hecksagon's own `attaches \"Governance\"`/`attaches \"Identity\"` pull them into this SAME compiled Store (hecks project_rust's own merged-registry step). Seeds three card payments (one left authorized, one captured-then-disputed, one authorized-and-tagged high_risk), two external transfers (`ExternalTransfer.Request` alone already lands \"sent\" — the ExternalSettlement process manager auto-dispatches Send on every request, unconditionally; the second is then Recalled, proving Sent excludes a transfer that moved past \"sent\"), a second, unrelated role assignment and role transition (proving both queries filter, not just list everything), and two external identifiers under the same issuer but different subjects (proving ResolvedBy's own two-clause `where` narrows to the exact pair asked for). Every `query` step below is a real declared query Ruby answers through `Runtime::QueryInterpreter#interpret`, not the ad hoc OBJECT-shaped filter (query_filters.json covers that shape) — this is the STRING form, args and all. The first two CardPayment.Authorize calls pass `tags: []` explicitly rather than omitting the (optional) argument, the same query_filters.json's own workaround for the same real, PRE-EXISTING, unrelated payload gap (Rust's typed-args-to_json always emits the key as null; Ruby's raw-args payload omits it entirely) — this fixture doesn't exist to prove or fix that either.",
